steps.body:
Sometimes you don't search a slice at all. You search for the smallest value that works, when every bigger value works too. Write a check for one guess, then binary search the range of possible answers: when `mid` works, keep it as a candidate with `hi = mid`, and otherwise go higher with `lo = mid + 1`.

Koko eats bananas at `speed` per hour, from one pile at a time, and has `h` hours:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

{{code}}

func main() {
	fmt.Println(canFinish([]int{3, 6, 7, 11}, 4, 8))
}
```

```go
func canFinish(piles []int, speed, h int) bool {
	hours := 0
	for _, p := range piles {
		hours += (p + speed - 1) / speed // p / speed, rounded up
	}
	return hours <= h
}
```

`(p + speed - 1) / speed` rounds up with no floats: a pile of 7 at speed 4 takes 2 hours. Speeds from 1 to the biggest pile are worth trying, since the biggest pile's speed finishes any pile in one hour:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func canFinish(piles []int, speed, h int) bool {
	hours := 0
	for _, p := range piles {
		hours += (p + speed - 1) / speed
	}
	return hours <= h
}

func main() {
	piles := []int{3, 6, 7, 11}
	h := 8
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(lo)
}
```

```go
lo, hi := 1, 11 // 11 is the biggest pile
for lo < hi {
	mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2
	if canFinish(piles, mid, h) {
		hi = mid // works: try slower
	} else {
		lo = mid + 1 // too slow
	}
}
```

The loop ends when `lo == hi`, the slowest speed that works, here 4. Never write `lo = mid`: `mid` rounds down, so the range could stop shrinking and loop forever.

